WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.021
Encoding must match the destination context.

2
00:00:03.021 --> 00:00:07.827
The browser interprets text differently depending on where it appears.

3
00:00:07.827 --> 00:00:17.164
HTML body text, attribute values, URL components, script data, style data, JSON, and client-side templates are not the same destination.

4
00:00:17.164 --> 00:00:23.275
A single generic encoding step cannot safely cover every place a value might be rendered.

5
00:00:23.275 --> 00:00:27.875
HTML body text and HTML attributes are related but still different.

6
00:00:27.875 --> 00:00:38.311
Framework-supported Razor and tag helper patterns usually handle common cases well when developers provide data as data rather than as assembled markup.

7
00:00:38.311 --> 00:00:49.845
Problems often appear when code concatenates strings into attributes, creates markup in helpers, or moves values between contexts without reconsidering the destination.

8
00:00:49.845 --> 00:00:54.445
JavaScript, CSS, URL, and JSON-related contexts need extra caution.

9
00:00:54.445 --> 00:01:00.487
The safest design is often to avoid placing untrusted data directly into risky contexts.

10
00:01:00.487 --> 00:01:16.072
For example, prefer passing structured data through safe framework mechanisms, using data attributes carefully, or letting a client framework bind text safely rather than mixing server strings into executable client-side areas.

11
00:01:16.072 --> 00:01:22.595
URL handling should distinguish between a full URL, a path, a query value, and a display label.

12
00:01:22.595 --> 00:01:26.646
CSS-related rendering should be kept narrow and controlled.

13
00:01:26.646 --> 00:01:31.520
JSON should be generated by serializers rather than hand-built strings.

14
00:01:31.520 --> 00:01:38.180
These distinctions matter because the browser does not interpret every destination as plain text.

15
00:01:38.180 --> 00:01:43.673
Prefer framework-supported helpers and encoders over custom string manipulation.

16
00:01:43.673 --> 00:01:50.813
Custom encoding code tends to miss context differences, edge cases, future changes, or team conventions.

17
00:01:50.813 --> 00:01:59.258
Approved platform patterns are easier to review and easier to keep consistent across modules, views, APIs, and client code.

18
00:01:59.258 --> 00:02:03.515
Review transitions where server data becomes client-side data.

19
00:02:03.515 --> 00:02:11.754
A value may start as a safe server-side string, become JSON, move into a client state store, and then appear in the DOM.

20
00:02:11.754 --> 00:02:16.628
Each transition should preserve the separation between data and markup.

21
00:02:16.628 --> 00:02:22.396
Context-aware encoding is a design discipline, not only a last-minute function call.
